      "text": "Let $x$ be the number of steps Ray climbs at a time. When Ray climbs $4$ steps at a time, there are $3$ steps left at the top, so we can write the congruence:\n\\[n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\\]\nSimilarly, when Ray climbs $5$ steps at a time, there are $2$ steps left at the top, so we can write the congruence:\n\\[n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}\\]\nWe are asked to find the smallest possible value of $n$ that is greater than $10$. The smallest positive integer that satisfies both congruences is $n = 13$. To verify, we can check that $13 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}$ and $13 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5}$.\nThus, the smallest possible value of $n$ that satisfies the given conditions is $\\boxed{13}$.The answer is: 13",
