      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest value of \\( n \\) that satisfies the given conditions when Ray climbs the stairs in two different ways: either 4 steps at a time or 5 steps at a time. The conditions are:\n\n1. When Ray climbs the stairs 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top.\n2. When Ray climbs the stairs 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top.\n\nWe can express these conditions mathematically with congruences:\n\\[ n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\]\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 10 \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), we can rewrite the congruence as:\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nWe can divide both sides by 4 (since 4 and 5 are coprime, 4 has an inverse modulo 5, which is 4):\n\\[ k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k = 5m + 1 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 4 + 3 = 20m + 7 \\]\nSo, \\( n \\) can be written as:\n\\[ n = 20m + 7 \\]\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n > 10 \\). If \\( m = 0 \\), then:\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 = 7 \\]\nIf \\( m = 1 \\), then:\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27 \\]\nWe can see that \\( n = 27 \\) is greater than 10. Therefore, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]",
