      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let's denote the number of steps in the flight of stairs as \\( n \\). We are given two conditions:\n1. When Ray climbs the stairs 4 steps at a time, there are 3 steps left at the top. This can be written as:\n\\[ n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\]\n2. When Ray climbs the stairs 5 steps at a time, there are 2 steps left at the top. This can be written as:\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies both congruences and is greater than 10. Let's start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Now we substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ 4k + 3 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ 4k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), we can rewrite the congruence as:\n\\[ 4k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nWe can divide both sides by 4, noting that 4 has a multiplicative inverse modulo 5, which is 4 (since \\(4 \\cdot 4 = 16 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\\)):\n\\[ k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[ k = 5m + 1 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\\[ n = 4k + 3 = 4(5m + 1) + 3 = 20m + 4 + 3 = 20m + 7 \\]\nSo, \\( n \\) can be written as:\n\\[ n = 20m + 7 \\]\nWe need the smallest value of \\( n \\) that is greater than 10. If \\( m = 0 \\), then:\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 0 + 7 = 7 \\]\nHowever, 7 is not greater than 10. If \\( m = 1 \\), then:\n\\[ n = 20 \\cdot 1 + 7 = 27 \\]\nSince 27 is greater than 10, the smallest possible value of \\( n \\) that satisfies both conditions is:\n\\[ \\boxed{27} \\]",
